e of Loss | Literary fiction | |\\n| 2007 | Anne Enright | The Gathering | Literary fiction | |\\n| 2008 | Aravind Adiga | The White Tiger | Literary fiction | |\\n| 2009 | Hilary Mantel | Wolf ... \",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Booker_Prize\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d29\"},\"ref\":\"d29\",\"snippet\":\" ... The White Tiger by Aravind Adiga -- My favorite book on this list; it was entertaining and unique, but not quite a great book.\\n\\nI have a couple other books on my bookshelf that I plan on reading at some point (The Famished Road, The Blind Assassin, Life & Times ... \",\"title\":\"https://www.reddit.com/r/literature/comments/189dqop/can_someone_explain_the_booker_prize/\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d30\"},\"ref\":\"d30\",\"snippet\":\" ... The final part, The Mirror and the Light , was published in March 2020.\\n\\nThe White Tiger, winner 2008 by Aravind Adiga The fortieth Booker winner, this was Aravind's debut novel and, at thirty-three, he was the second youngest author to win the prize. It tackles themes ... \",\"title\":\"https://www.panmacmillan.com/blogs/literary/our-all-time-favourite-man-booker-prize-winning-novels\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d31\"},\"ref\":\"d31\",\"snippet\":\" ... In 2008, Free Press published The White Tiger, Indian author Aravind Adiga's debut novel, which won the Man Booker Prize.\\n\\nNotable books\\n\\n* Carl Menger (1950). Principles of Economics. Glencoe, IL: Free Press\\n* Talcott Parsons (1951). The Social System. Glencoe, IL: Free Press\\n* Elihu Katz and Paul Lazarsfeld ... \",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_Press_(publisher)\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d32\"},\"ref\":\"d32\",\"snippet\":\" ... Cruella says it best herself: \\\"I was born brilliant, born bad, and a little bit mad.\\\"\\n\\n4. 'The White Tiger'\\n\\nAn adaptation of Aravind Adiga's Booker Prize-winning book of the same name, Balram Halwai (played by Adarsh Gourav) is the titular \\\"white tiger\\\" born once every ... \",\"title\":\"https://www.marieclaire.com/culture/g35543620/best-movies-based-on-books-2021/\"}],\"next_step\":\"Inspect navigation; read a relevant page or change the locating clue.\",\"observation\":{\"first_delivered_source_round\":null,\"prior_deliveries\":0,\"query_key\":\"8d16215ec3a6489a5c585117c6d24c79000c33d2a9a9fec2e6fa7b848e992f59\",\"replay_requested\":false,\"result_fingerprint\":\"59f8f482db381140203aed844f13004506ef1b153e7b42b6dbb0f53f6b838764\",\"semantic_progress\":\"not_measured\"},\"query\":\"The White Tiger Aravind Adiga translated languages Booker Prize\",\"view\":\"full\"}]}","role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_695f54220f2446d08ae50bc4"},{"content":"Let me reconsider the puzzle. The Man Booker International Prize was inaugurated in 2005 (2000s). Let me look at the winners and shortlisted books. The key is finding a book translated into 25+ languages that was a contender (shortlisted) for this prize.","role":"assistant","tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"ref\": \"d21\"}","name":"read"},"id":"call_2f45c00167f442b587a221d2","index":0,"type":"function"}]},{"content":"{\"action_slot_charged\":true,\"evidence\":{\"document\":\"d21\",\"document_chars\":27249,\"end\":3000,\"kind\":\"raw_evidence\",\"ref\":\"e1\",\"sha256\":\"8ce61c1087f7e5edc11d95aa8c819ec764b2f8d2e4c5ab64aaa81fda45d083cf\",\"snapshot\":\"25e42c9d9629a06063929aefe4e8f050cac2c095d797b7476410c1a6fe277c5a\",\"start\":0,\"text\":\"---\\ntitle: Booker Prize - Wikipedia\\nauthor: Authority control databases National Germany United States France BnF data Czech Republic Israel\\ndate: 2001-10-18\\n---\\nname: The Booker Prize\\nawarded_for: Best work of sustained fiction of the year, written in English and published in the UK or Ireland\\nreward: £50, 000\\npresenter: Booker, McConnell Ltd (1969–2001), Man Group (2002–2019), Crankstart (2019 onwards)\\nlocation: Somerset House, Strand, London, England\\nyear: 1969\\nimagesize: 150px\\n\\nThe Booker Prize, formerly the Booker Prize for Fiction (1969–2001) and the Man Booker Prize (2002–2019), is a prestigious literary award conferred each year for the best single work of sustained fiction written in the English language, which was published in the United Kingdom or Ireland. The winner of the Booker Prize receives , as well as international publicity that usually leads to a significant sales boost. When the prize was created, only novels written by Commonwealth, Irish and South African (and later Zimbabwean) citizens were eligible to receive the prize; in 2014, eligibility was widened to any English-language novel—a change that proved controversial.\\n\\nA five-person panel consisting of authors, publishers and journalists, as well as politicians, actors, artists and musicians, is appointed by the Booker Prize Foundation each year to choose the winning book. Gaby Wood has been the chief executive of the Booker Prize Foundation since 2015.Gaby Wood at The Booker Prizes.Jennifer (20 April 2015), \\\"Booker Prize Foundation Hints at New Direction with Appointment of Gaby Wood as Literary Director\\\", Books Live, Sunday Times (South Africa).\\n\\nA high-profile literary award in British culture, the Booker Prize is greeted with anticipation and fanfare around the world. Literary critics have noted that it is a mark of distinction for authors to be selected for inclusion in the shortlist or to be nominated for the \\\"longlist\\\".\\n\\nA sister prize, the International Booker Prize, is awarded for a work of fiction translated into English and published in the United Kingdom or Ireland. Unlike the Booker Prize, short story collections are eligible for the International Booker Prize. The £50,000 prize money is split evenly between the author and translator of the winning novel.\\n\\nHistory and administration\\n\\nThe prize was established as the \\\"Booker Prize for Fiction\\\" after the company Booker, McConnell Ltd began sponsoring the event in 1969; it became commonly known as the \\\"Booker Prize\\\" or the \\\"Booker\\\". Jock Campbell, Charles Tyrrell and Tom Maschler were instrumental in establishing the prize.\\n\\nWhen administration of the prize was transferred to the Booker Prize Foundation in 2002, the title sponsor became the investment company Man Group, which opted to retain \\\"Booker\\\" as part of the official title of the prize. The foundation is an independent registered charity funded by the entire profits of Booker Prize Trading Ltd, of which it is the sole shareholder. The prize money awarded w\"},\"executed\":true,\"next_start\":3000,\"ok\":true,\"previously_received\":false}","role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_2f45c00167f442b587a221d2"},{"content":"Let me reconsider the puzzle structure. The award was created in the 2000s. The Man Booker International Prize was inaugurated in 2005. Let me look at the winners and shortlisted books. The key is finding a book translated into 25+ languages that was a contender for this prize. Let me search for the Man Booker International Prize shortlists and winners.","role":"assistant","tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"queries\": [\"Man Booker International Prize 2011 2013 2015 shortlist winner\"]}","name":"search"},"id":"call_b81a6378e34e48bc99339f0a","index":0,"type":"function"},{"function":{"arguments":"{\"queries\": [\"book translated into 26 languages shortlisted Man Booker International Prize\"]}","name":"search"},"id":"call_1d69332be1e04b6aa643c772","index":1,"type":"function"}]},{"content":"{\"action_slot_char